# Things to read while the install runs.
#
# One file, both front ends. The graphical installer fades between these in a
# card under the progress list and the text installer rotates them on a line
# under the bar, and neither owns the words, because two installers telling a
# person different things about the same system is how a product starts to
# feel like several products.
#
# Format is lane, tab, text. Lanes:
#
#   tip    something true about the system being installed right now
#   next   something to have ready for after the restart
#   rare   shown roughly one rotation in forty
#   long   shown instead of the rotation once the install passes twenty
#          minutes, and not given back
#
# Rules for writing one. It has to be true of this install, not of operating
# systems in general, because a fact the reader can check is worth ten that
# sound nice. It has to fit on two lines of the text installer, which is 120
# characters, and tests/test-progress-wait.sh will say so if it does not. It is read by somebody with nothing to do for ten minutes,
# so it can be interesting, and it is read by somebody who is nervous about
# their disk, so it is never cute about that. No exclamation marks.
#
# The `long` lane is not a joke lane either, and it is the one place in this
# product where the reader is worried. Twenty minutes in, the question is no
# longer "what is Btrfs", it is "has this stopped". Answer that, say thank
# you, and do not be clever.
#
# The `rare` lane is not a joke lane. It is the one that is allowed to be warm
# about the fact that a person is sitting there waiting, and it shows up seldom
# enough that finding one feels like finding something.
#
# These strings go through tests/test-voice.sh with everything else.

tip	Your files will live on Btrfs, which can take a snapshot of the whole system in about a second.
tip	A snapshot does not copy anything. It records what changes after it, so it costs nothing until something does.
tip	A recovery snapshot is saved before the first restart. It is a way back to exactly how the system leaves here.
tip	The boot menu will have a rollback entry. It starts the system from that snapshot and changes nothing on the way.
tip	Rolling back is a boot menu choice, not a repair. Nothing is rewritten, so a rollback that does not help can be undone.
tip	Every package was downloaded and checked before the disk was touched. This install no longer needs the network.
tip	Packages come from one pinned day of the Arch archive, not from whatever is newest, so two machines get the same system.
tip	AuraDE starts with systemd-boot rather than GRUB. Its boot entries are plain text files you can fix with an editor.
tip	If you turned on encryption, the disk is protected with LUKS2 and unreadable to anything without the passphrase.
tip	Encryption covers the whole system, not a folder inside it. No unencrypted copy of anything is left behind.
tip	The disk passphrase is checked by the bootloader, before the system starts. That is why it is asked for so early.
tip	Swap is where memory goes when there is not enough. A swap file can be added later, so it need not be decided now.
tip	Hibernating writes all of memory out to disk, which is why a hibernation swap file has to be at least that large.
tip	This installer never resizes a partition. It only uses free space, which is why it can say what it will do beforehand.
tip	The installer writes a journal as it goes. That file is what the progress list on this screen is drawn from.
tip	AuraDE signs its boot files when a Secure Boot key is available. Without one, turn Secure Boot off before restarting.
tip	The EFI partition is the part of the disk the firmware reads. It is small, plain FAT, and any computer can mount it.
tip	Everything here is true of the text installer too. They ask the same questions and run the same engine.
tip	Your account is created during this step, with the username and password you chose. The live session has neither.
tip	The time zone you picked sets the clock and nothing else. It can be changed later without reinstalling.
tip	Your keyboard layout is written into both the system and the bootloader, so the passphrase prompt uses the same keys.

next	Take out the installation media before restarting. Some computers will boot straight back into this installer.
next	Sign in with the username and password you typed a few screens ago. They are not the disk passphrase.
next	If you turned on encryption, the disk passphrase is asked for first, before the sign-in screen.
next	The first start takes a little longer than the ones after it. Nothing is wrong.
next	The rollback entry in the boot menu points at the snapshot being saved today.
next	Nothing needs downloading after the restart. The system that starts is the one written here.

rare	Somebody chose the colour of this bar on purpose. Thank you for waiting long enough to notice.
rare	This screen was built by people who have watched a lot of installers and been bored by all of them.
rare	Ten minutes is about four songs, or the time it takes tea to go cold. The install does not mind which.
rare	If you are reading every one of these, the install is going fine and you have excellent patience.
rare	There is a small game on this screen. It is not hidden very well.

